1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
We eat over 10 billion pounds of it each year.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
It satiated the ancients and built modern-day empires.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:20,000
From the equatorial fields into famous factories, from bonbons to bunnies, pods to peanut butter cups.

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:29,000
At the heart of this giant industry is a little magic bean, believed to be an energizer, aphrodisiac, and cure-all.

5
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,000
Now, Chocolate on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Bars.

7
00:00:45,001 --> 00:00:46,000
Ice cream.

8
00:00:46,001 --> 00:00:47,000
Cookies.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Cake.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Candy.

11
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:52,000
Santas.

12
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Hearts.

13
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:55,000
Bunnies.

14
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:56,000
Eggs.

15
00:00:56,001 --> 00:00:57,000
Hollow.

16
00:00:57,001 --> 00:00:58,000
Filled.

17
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:02,820
Hot, cold, solid, liquid, powder.

18
00:01:02,821 --> 00:01:06,580
Chocolate comes in many forms and has legions of fans.

19
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:17,120
Annual sales in the US top $16 billion and $75 billion worldwide.

20
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:25,220
The average American consumes 12 pounds per year.

21
00:01:25,221 --> 00:01:29,960
The sweet toothed Swiss, an astounding 22 pounds per person each year.

22
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:39,240
Millions of people engage in the production of chocolate so that millions more can revel in their passion for it.

23
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:51,160
One of the companies that capitalizes on this passion is Mars Incorporated, maker of M&M's.

24
00:01:51,161 --> 00:01:55,340
Welcome to the M&M's factory in Hackettstown, New Jersey.

25
00:01:56,940 --> 00:01:58,880
And all we make here is M&M's.

26
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:01,360
We have several varieties of M&M's.

27
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:02,960
We have milk chocolate.

28
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:04,340
We used to call it plain.

29
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:06,420
But that seemed too simple.

30
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:07,440
We have peanut.

31
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:08,640
We have almond.

32
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:10,360
And we have peanut butter.

33
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,440
And they produce several million of them per day.

34
00:02:15,441 --> 00:02:26,200
The process begins as milk chocolate is refined and blended for the optimal flavor, texture,

35
00:02:26,201 --> 00:02:28,200
and melting point.

36
00:02:28,201 --> 00:02:33,440
We've made the chocolate, now it's time to make the M&Ms, and this is the first step.

37
00:02:33,441 --> 00:02:36,920
The chocolate is being deposited on chilled rollers.

38
00:02:36,921 --> 00:02:41,580
Each of those rollers have dimples on them, so the chocolate's being pressed into it,

39
00:02:41,581 --> 00:02:47,280
And the output is a wall of dimples that go through a cooling tunnel and on into the next

40
00:02:47,281 --> 00:02:52,200
step of gunship.

41
00:02:52,201 --> 00:02:57,200
The cooled chocolate pellets are transferred to these giant rotating pans, where they roll

42
00:02:57,201 --> 00:03:01,900
against each other and start to take on the familiar shape, prior to the application of

43
00:03:01,901 --> 00:03:04,020
the signature outer coating.

44
00:03:04,021 --> 00:03:08,520
Each of these pans hold about a thousand pounds.

45
00:03:08,521 --> 00:03:13,520
And what happens is that you start to see the beginning of an M&F.

46
00:03:13,521 --> 00:03:16,520
This is an opportunity for us to do a quality check.

47
00:03:16,521 --> 00:03:18,520
We're looking at the weight, we're looking at the size.

48
00:03:18,521 --> 00:03:23,520
As we go into the sugar shell, you have to have a uniform M&F.

49
00:03:25,520 --> 00:03:30,520
A mixture made with liquid sucrose, cornstarch, and coloring is poured into the pans,

50
00:03:30,521 --> 00:03:33,520
enveloping each pellet as it hardens.

51
00:03:33,521 --> 00:03:42,200
Next, the sugar-coated candy travels to the printing area.

52
00:03:42,201 --> 00:03:48,600
Each piece sits snugly in its slot, passing under a printing roller, bearing the signature

53
00:03:48,601 --> 00:03:53,820
M in an edible white dye.

54
00:03:53,821 --> 00:03:58,240
Mars can even custom print your M&Ms, with everything from marriage proposals to the

55
00:03:58,241 --> 00:04:04,040
logo of your favorite TV network.

56
00:04:04,041 --> 00:04:07,880
Finally, individual conveyors carrying a single color of candy

57
00:04:07,881 --> 00:04:10,440
dump onto a central belt on the road to packaging.

58
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:17,140
But no matter what's on the shelf,

59
00:04:17,141 --> 00:04:19,840
it's the chocolate inside that's the real treat.

60
00:04:19,841 --> 00:04:30,020
All chocolate starts life as a seed, or bean, inside the fruit of the cacao tree.

61
00:04:30,021 --> 00:04:33,900
There are three kinds of cocoa beans.

62
00:04:33,901 --> 00:04:41,140
There are the criollos that were originated in New Venezuela and cultivated in Mesoamerica.

63
00:04:41,141 --> 00:04:47,880
The criollos have a very good flavor, but they may not be very sturdy, very aggressive

64
00:04:47,881 --> 00:04:49,160
or resistant to diseases.

65
00:04:49,161 --> 00:04:55,840
Then we have the Amazonicus or the Forasteros that are very sturdy, very resistant, very

66
00:04:55,841 --> 00:04:56,840
highly productive.

67
00:04:56,841 --> 00:05:02,540
And there is a mixture of these two that is called Trinitarian.

68
00:05:02,541 --> 00:05:11,700
The cacao tree grows primarily in regions within 10 degrees north or south of the equator.

69
00:05:11,701 --> 00:05:18,620
It's a finicky plant that requires rich soil, steady rainfall and tropical heat, but also

70
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:26,380
shade from the tropical sun and shelter from the wind.

71
00:05:26,381 --> 00:05:31,040
It takes five to six months for the cacao pods to ripen, allowing for harvest twice

72
00:05:31,041 --> 00:05:34,260
a year.

73
00:05:34,261 --> 00:05:41,060
Here in Costa Rica and around the world, farmers harvest the pods by hand, a practice unchanged

74
00:05:41,061 --> 00:05:44,300
for over 2,000 years.

75
00:05:44,301 --> 00:05:52,060
could damage the soil, delicate trees and pods.

76
00:05:52,061 --> 00:05:58,320
They put these pods in this pile and everybody sits around to break the pods.

77
00:05:58,321 --> 00:06:00,460
Here you are.

78
00:06:00,461 --> 00:06:06,240
These are the beans and have this flesh, this pulp, which is sweet and sour, very good for

79
00:06:06,241 --> 00:06:07,240
taste.

80
00:06:07,241 --> 00:06:10,360
You can eat it, you know, you can...

81
00:06:10,361 --> 00:06:18,280
The pulp may be sweet, but the beans inside are quite bitter.

82
00:06:18,281 --> 00:06:23,620
An experienced worker can crack open up to 500 pods an hour.

83
00:06:23,621 --> 00:06:30,640
Each pod will yield about 30 to 40 beans.

84
00:06:30,641 --> 00:06:32,600
Next the beans need to ferment.

85
00:06:32,601 --> 00:06:37,920
If the farm isn't near a processing facility, workers use the traditional method of laying

86
00:06:37,921 --> 00:06:43,680
piles of beans between banana leaves to keep out dirt and rain and to insulate

87
00:06:43,681 --> 00:06:52,980
against heat loss. During the fermentation process yeast metabolizes

88
00:06:52,981 --> 00:06:59,880
the sugars in the pulp producing heat and ethanol as byproducts. Bacteria

89
00:06:59,881 --> 00:07:06,060
consume the ethanol and produce lactic and acetic acids. The alcohol and acids

90
00:07:06,061 --> 00:07:10,560
penetrate the shell and together with the heat create a reaction that allows

91
00:07:10,561 --> 00:07:14,660
the cocoa flavor of the beans to develop and some of the bitterness to subside.

92
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:25,220
After fermenting for about six days the beans are ready to be dried in the sun.

93
00:07:25,221 --> 00:07:38,400
Here we have the cacao beans drying in the sun.

94
00:07:38,401 --> 00:07:42,980
These have been drying for two days and need at least five more days to reach a moisture

95
00:07:42,981 --> 00:07:48,260
level of 7%.

96
00:07:48,261 --> 00:07:55,260
The advantage is that they will have a nice aroma and flavor.

97
00:07:56,440 --> 00:08:01,440
The less moisture in the beans, the less likely they are to grow mold during shipment.

98
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:12,440
The dried beans are then scrutinized for quality, bagged and weighed.

99
00:08:12,441 --> 00:08:22,700
It takes roughly 1,600 to 1,700 pods, or 60,000 beans, to fill up a bag that can cost anywhere from $150 to $800.

100
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:34,260
Now the sacks are ready to be shipped worldwide to chocolate manufacturers, like Guitard Chocolate in Burlingame, California.

101
00:08:34,261 --> 00:08:41,020
Kitard provides chocolate in bulk quantities to companies and professionals that will make

102
00:08:41,021 --> 00:08:43,380
the products we all crave.

103
00:08:43,381 --> 00:08:52,560
The products that a lot of our customers make are candy, bonbons or your traditional truffle.

104
00:08:52,561 --> 00:08:54,360
Some people make candy bars.

105
00:08:54,361 --> 00:09:00,360
Fine restaurants will make chocolate molten cakes and mousses.

106
00:09:00,361 --> 00:09:04,360
We use our chocolate chips for making cookies.

107
00:09:04,361 --> 00:09:09,360
Guittard creates chocolate formulas unique to each type of product.

108
00:09:09,361 --> 00:09:12,360
And it all starts with a bean.

109
00:09:12,361 --> 00:09:20,360
Beans come to us from all over the world, from Africa, Central America, South America, and the Far East.

110
00:09:20,361 --> 00:09:26,360
And we select the flavors that we're interested in making for each specific chocolate.

111
00:09:26,361 --> 00:09:30,540
Each region of the world produces a particular flavor.

112
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:36,540
These beans are what we refer to as a raw agricultural commodity.

113
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:41,800
Nobody on the face of the earth will ever put these into their mouth in this condition.

114
00:09:42,820 --> 00:09:49,640
They're dusty, there is stuff in them, and the first job is to clean these beans.

115
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:55,740
The beans travel through a hopper to the cleaning area.

116
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:59,700
where forced air, vibration, and then a vacuum

117
00:09:59,701 --> 00:10:03,360
will start to remove the foreign material.

118
00:10:03,361 --> 00:10:10,200
After we clean the beans, we come up to what we call the de-stoner.

119
00:10:10,201 --> 00:10:16,620
As the name implies, its job is to remove stones.

120
00:10:16,621 --> 00:10:21,360
Our milling equipment does not like rocks very much.

121
00:10:21,361 --> 00:10:25,120
It does a fair amount of damage, so this is critical.

122
00:10:25,121 --> 00:10:26,700
But there's one other piece.

123
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:33,980
You have to remember that where beans are grown in the world are jungle conditions.

124
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:39,300
This is a very wild place, including wild animals.

125
00:10:40,280 --> 00:10:42,760
You don't go out into the jungle unprotected.

126
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:49,740
One of the things we pick up in this D-stoner is we also pick up live ammunition.

127
00:10:49,741 --> 00:10:59,680
We consider it really good form to remove all live ammo before we roast the cocoa beans for some fairly obvious reasons.

128
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:05,360
The cleaned, unloaded beans are loaded into the roaster.

129
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:10,260
The roasting process can take anywhere from 10 to 30 minutes.

130
00:11:11,460 --> 00:11:14,160
From here, we have to remove the shell.

131
00:11:14,161 --> 00:11:20,860
So it goes into a machine we call the winnower, which breaks the bean apart, removes the shell,

132
00:11:20,861 --> 00:11:23,100
and we are left with a pure nib.

133
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:24,540
This is the heart.

134
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:26,380
This is what we're really after.

135
00:11:29,740 --> 00:11:34,200
The nibs are pre-milled into a coarse paste called chocolate liquor.

136
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:40,920
Then onto the triple stone mill for finer grinding.

137
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:49,400
The mill consists of three pairs of stone grinders.

138
00:11:49,401 --> 00:11:53,900
Coarse chocolate liquor is pumped to the first stone pair.

139
00:11:53,901 --> 00:11:58,600
The top stone rotates and grinds the liquor as it is forced toward the outer edges and

140
00:11:58,601 --> 00:12:01,200
collected.

141
00:12:01,201 --> 00:12:06,320
The mass flows down a chute to the next stone pair, where it's ground even finer.

142
00:12:06,321 --> 00:12:11,000
A third pass and then out the bottom.

143
00:12:11,001 --> 00:12:17,900
The cocoa particle size is now one-sixth the diameter of a human hair.

144
00:12:17,901 --> 00:12:22,000
The resulting liquid chocolate liquor is divided.

145
00:12:22,001 --> 00:12:32,720
A portion goes to the cocoa press to have the cocoa butter literally squeezed out of it.

146
00:12:32,721 --> 00:12:37,720
Chocolate liquor is loaded into vertical pots inside the cocoa press.

147
00:12:37,721 --> 00:12:40,880
using hydraulic pressure of up to 8,000 pounds PSI.

148
00:12:41,600 --> 00:12:44,880
The main ram slowly pushes pistons into each pot,

149
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:46,460
compressing the liquor.

150
00:12:47,900 --> 00:12:50,400
Most of the fat is released and collected below,

151
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:53,620
and you're left with cocoa powder cakes.

152
00:12:54,680 --> 00:12:57,580
We will grind it up and it will become cocoa powder

153
00:12:57,581 --> 00:13:00,840
that you use in baking a cake or some brownies at home.

154
00:13:03,300 --> 00:13:05,780
Then it's back to the other portion of chocolate liquor

155
00:13:05,781 --> 00:13:07,460
that hasn't had its fat removed.

156
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:16,360
The liquor is combined with sugar or sugar and milk powder, depending on the desired

157
00:13:16,361 --> 00:13:18,360
end product.

158
00:13:18,361 --> 00:13:26,740
A series of steel rollers crushes and refines the thick mixture into a flaky substance.

159
00:13:26,741 --> 00:13:34,500
Then, the conch, essentially a giant mixer, helps distribute the fat and develop the

160
00:13:34,501 --> 00:13:38,000
texture as the liquid ingredients are integrated.

161
00:13:38,001 --> 00:13:44,680
It typically will add somewhere around 28 to 32 pounds of lecithin for 10,000 pounds

162
00:13:44,681 --> 00:13:50,380
of chocolate, and then we'll add additional cocoa butter.

163
00:13:50,381 --> 00:13:58,880
The soy lecithin acts as an emulsifier, helping the wet and dry ingredients mix well.

164
00:13:58,881 --> 00:14:03,000
Incorporating the extra cocoa butter creates a less viscous blend that will make the chocolate

165
00:14:03,001 --> 00:14:03,831
creamier.

166
00:14:03,832 --> 00:14:11,960
From the conches we're going to go to a set of tanks and from the tanks we send it to be

167
00:14:11,961 --> 00:14:22,120
deposited as 10 pound bars, as wafers, as cookie rocks or possibly not to be deposited at all but

168
00:14:22,121 --> 00:14:27,203
to be sent out as a 50,000 pound liquid tanker full of chocolate.

169
00:14:27,204 --> 00:14:29,080
All this good stuff from

170
00:14:29,081 --> 00:14:31,580
from processing a seed grown in the rainforest.

171
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:38,520
This symbiosis of manpower and machine power

172
00:14:38,521 --> 00:14:42,040
has spawned an industry that satisfies a worldwide craving

173
00:14:42,041 --> 00:14:45,240
two millennia old.

174
00:14:45,241 --> 00:14:49,020
A craving born not just of taste, but of the basic desires

175
00:14:49,021 --> 00:14:50,780
for sex and money.

176
00:14:56,300 --> 00:14:58,720
In the average bag of milk chocolate M&Ms,

177
00:14:58,721 --> 00:15:06,100
You'll find 24% blue, 20% orange, 16% green, 14% yellow,

178
00:15:06,101 --> 00:15:11,220
13% red, and 13% brown colored candy shells.

179
00:15:11,221 --> 00:15:14,260
Chocolate will return on Modern Marvels,

180
00:15:14,261 --> 00:15:16,100
here on the History Channel.

181
00:15:19,300 --> 00:15:22,240
We now return to Chocolate on Modern Marvels.

182
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:28,680
At Max Brenner Chocolate by the Bald Man,

183
00:15:28,681 --> 00:15:30,460
in New York City.

184
00:15:30,461 --> 00:15:33,160
Chocolate can be a refined affair

185
00:15:33,161 --> 00:15:35,440
or an all out gluttonous free for all.

186
00:15:38,300 --> 00:15:39,800
Chocolate pizza, anyone?

187
00:15:43,280 --> 00:15:44,860
How about a syringe full of chocolate

188
00:15:44,861 --> 00:15:46,440
squirted right into your mouth?

189
00:15:48,140 --> 00:15:50,340
Dieters, beware.

190
00:15:50,341 --> 00:15:53,380
Max Brenner is creating a new chocolate culture.

191
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:58,660
We use this very special food ingredient, chocolate,

192
00:15:58,661 --> 00:16:05,920
to give each person who comes here a very intimate and personal experience.

193
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:12,080
Renner's personal chocophilia rivals that of historic fanatics.

194
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:17,740
If we look many years backwards to the time of the Aztec,

195
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:20,880
the king of the Aztec, who was like the god,

196
00:16:20,881 --> 00:16:24,180
he was allowed to eat this food

197
00:16:24,181 --> 00:16:28,660
because they said that it has all these very unique virtues.

198
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:32,900
Today, we can all be kings because we can all eat chocolate.

199
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:39,580
The current state of chocoholism traces back to the Mesoamerican cultures.

200
00:16:42,620 --> 00:16:46,440
The classic Maya civilization of Central America and Southern Mexico,

201
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:49,120
and the Aztecs of Central Mexico,

202
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:53,620
and possibly even as far back as the Olmec civilization.

203
00:16:54,180 --> 00:17:03,880
During ceremonies and celebrations, Maya and Aztec warriors, priests, and nobility drank

204
00:17:03,881 --> 00:17:13,080
a hot potion of ground-roasted cocoa beans and water, to which spices were often added.

205
00:17:13,081 --> 00:17:20,020
They called the bitter drink, chocolatl.

206
00:17:20,021 --> 00:17:24,700
Oladad Lopez of Aztec descent uses old world tools and techniques to make

207
00:17:24,701 --> 00:17:27,400
chocolatl for her Los Angeles, California restaurant.

208
00:17:43,340 --> 00:17:46,680
After 20 minutes of slow roasting over charcoal,

209
00:17:46,681 --> 00:17:53,840
The Aztecs peeled the darkened beans and painstakingly ground them 20 separate times on a limestone matate.

210
00:17:55,520 --> 00:18:00,020
Usually it takes about eight hours.

211
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:08,260
And usually done only by women and little girls because it has to be very patient.

212
00:18:10,020 --> 00:18:14,980
The resulting thick paste was formed into measured cakes and set aside to dry.

213
00:18:16,680 --> 00:18:19,440
Three cakes are added per liter of hot water

214
00:18:19,441 --> 00:18:22,400
and then crushed with a wood molineo.

215
00:18:25,180 --> 00:18:27,960
It was the frothy elixir of Aztec life

216
00:18:27,961 --> 00:18:31,560
and sometimes a taste of the bitter end.

217
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:33,620
It had symbolic reference

218
00:18:33,621 --> 00:18:37,200
because the cacao pud is sort of the shape of a human heart.

219
00:18:37,980 --> 00:18:40,360
And, of course, the Aztecs, we know,

220
00:18:40,460 --> 00:18:43,200
were enormously interested in heart extraction

221
00:18:43,201 --> 00:18:45,700
during sacrifices.

222
00:18:46,680 --> 00:18:52,720
They were also interested in chocolate as an aphrodisiac.

223
00:18:54,160 --> 00:19:00,040
Legend has it that Aztec ruler Montezuma consumed 50 goblets of chocolatel per day,

224
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:03,540
usually right before he entered his harem.

225
00:19:04,760 --> 00:19:07,100
Which is ridiculous. Nobody could do that.

226
00:19:07,101 --> 00:19:08,198
It's a diuretic.

227
00:19:08,199 --> 00:19:14,440
He'd have to go to the bathroom very quickly because of the caffeine and theobromane in it.

228
00:19:14,441 --> 00:19:20,820
So maybe 50 goblets a day isn't historically accurate, but for centuries people have believed

229
00:19:20,821 --> 00:19:24,520
that chocolate puts you in the mood and increases sexual stamina.

230
00:19:24,521 --> 00:19:32,540
It does contain chemicals that influence blood flow and certainly amorous activity

231
00:19:32,541 --> 00:19:36,360
and the redistribution of blood in the body go together.

232
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:43,020
And there are chemicals in chocolate that likely improve mood as well.

233
00:19:43,021 --> 00:19:47,700
one of the likely candidates is an amino acid called tryptophan,

234
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:53,720
which is the precursor to a chemical in the brain called serotonin, which enhances mood.

235
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:57,380
In any case, cocoa was valuable.

236
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:01,140
The Maya and Aztecs even used the beans as currency.

237
00:20:02,340 --> 00:20:04,180
A rabbit cost 4 to 30 beans.

238
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:05,840
A mule, 50.

239
00:20:06,460 --> 00:20:09,300
A turkey or slave, 100 beans.

240
00:20:13,020 --> 00:20:17,920
There is some debate over who brought cocoa beans and the recipe for

241
00:20:17,921 --> 00:20:20,620
chocolate back to Spain in the 16th century.

242
00:20:22,220 --> 00:20:26,360
Columbus, Cortes, or Mayan emissaries.

243
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:31,480
The Spanish added sugar to the bitter drink to appease their delicate palates.

244
00:20:33,020 --> 00:20:36,720
A big hit with the country's elite, chocolate was a jealously guarded secret.

245
00:20:36,721 --> 00:20:45,400
The secret was so well kept, in fact, that 16th century English pirates who captured

246
00:20:45,401 --> 00:20:51,240
a Spanish treasure ship were perplexed to find what looked like a cargo of dried sheep's

247
00:20:51,241 --> 00:20:52,660
droppings.

248
00:20:52,661 --> 00:20:57,560
They burned the entire ship in anger.

249
00:20:57,561 --> 00:21:04,620
Little did they know that in Spain, the beans were worth their weight in silver.

250
00:21:04,621 --> 00:21:09,480
It took nearly a century, but eventually the proverbial beans were spilled.

251
00:21:09,481 --> 00:21:16,800
And everyone in Europe went mad for sweet chocolate.

252
00:21:16,801 --> 00:21:22,920
To ensure a constant supply of sugar, and now cocoa, colonists built an ill-gotten labor

253
00:21:22,921 --> 00:21:26,840
force in the growing regions of Central America and the Caribbean.

254
00:21:26,841 --> 00:21:30,820
The native Indian peoples were dying off from introduced diseases.

255
00:21:30,821 --> 00:21:37,700
They simply weren't resistant to smallpox and other terrible European or Old World diseases.

256
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:39,820
So they had a diminished labor force.

257
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:43,600
To make up for this, they got into the African slave trade.

258
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:50,580
And hundreds of thousands, eventually millions, of Africans were torn out of Africa.

259
00:21:51,960 --> 00:21:56,880
Back in Europe, due to high import taxes, chocolate was hard on the wallet.

260
00:21:56,881 --> 00:22:01,640
And due to the high fat content in the beans, it was hard on the stomach too.

261
00:22:04,840 --> 00:22:11,560
In 1828, a simple machine made it a bit easier to swallow and digest.

262
00:22:11,561 --> 00:22:18,440
The cocoa press squeezed about half of the natural fat from the cocoa solids.

263
00:22:18,441 --> 00:22:24,640
The resulting cake could be pulverized into a powder and added to water or milk.

264
00:22:34,951 --> 00:22:40,866
In 1847, Mr. Fry in England was able to produce a first solid edible chocolate bar.

265
00:22:40,867 --> 00:22:45,640
Fry took the cocoa butter and added it with cocoa powder and sugar.

266
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:51,640
This recipe eliminated water from the equation, allowing the product to harden.

267
00:22:51,641 --> 00:23:00,600
The bar was bitter and brittle by today's standards, but it was a start.

268
00:23:00,601 --> 00:23:05,200
Chocolate makers searched for ways to make the bars more palatable.

269
00:23:05,201 --> 00:23:13,540
The answer was milk, but it didn't mix well with the cocoa powder-cocoa butter combination.

270
00:23:13,541 --> 00:23:20,600
Because milk is nearly 90% water, it was kind of like mixing oil and water.

271
00:23:20,601 --> 00:23:26,720
In 1879, Swiss chocolatiers created the first milk chocolate by incorporating sweetened,

272
00:23:26,721 --> 00:23:30,520
evaporated milk.

273
00:23:30,521 --> 00:23:36,680
The 19th and early 20th centuries saw a huge chocolate boom, with today's familiar company

274
00:23:36,681 --> 00:23:45,280
names like Cadbury, Lindt, Hershey, and Mars coming into existence.

275
00:23:45,281 --> 00:23:50,560
Luckily for these industry giants, the taste for the brown bean had spread, and consumer

276
00:23:50,561 --> 00:23:54,360
appetite was about to become insatiable.

277
00:23:54,361 --> 00:24:03,140
The theobromine in chocolate is toxic to dogs because they can't digest it as efficiently

278
00:24:03,141 --> 00:24:05,100
as humans.

279
00:24:05,101 --> 00:24:12,400
It can cause heart rhythm abnormalities, muscle tremors, seizures, and in large quantities

280
00:24:12,401 --> 00:24:14,660
even death.

281
00:24:14,661 --> 00:24:16,900
will return on Modern Marvels.

282
00:24:20,320 --> 00:24:23,260
We now return to Chocolate on Modern Marvels.

283
00:24:28,300 --> 00:24:31,080
We've been chocolate addicts since the very first bars

284
00:24:31,081 --> 00:24:33,180
hit stores in the mid 19th century.

285
00:24:36,360 --> 00:24:40,500
Today, one can find just about anything covered in chocolate.

286
00:24:40,501 --> 00:24:49,620
Fruit, nuts, nougat, pretzels, caramel, cookies, peanut butter,

287
00:24:49,621 --> 00:24:52,680
even worms and crickets.

288
00:24:52,681 --> 00:24:57,260
In the US, the undisputed kings of covering stuff in chocolate

289
00:24:57,261 --> 00:24:58,620
are Hershey and Mars.

290
00:25:01,800 --> 00:25:04,420
To make its signature Hershey bars and Hershey bars

291
00:25:04,421 --> 00:25:09,660
with almonds, Hershey blends sugar, milk, cocoa butter,

292
00:25:09,661 --> 00:25:14,200
chocolate liquor, lecithin, and vanilla.

293
00:25:14,201 --> 00:25:17,340
The chocolate mixture is then heated, cooled, and agitated

294
00:25:17,341 --> 00:25:21,560
in a process known as tempering.

295
00:25:21,561 --> 00:25:23,880
Similar to glass or metal tempering,

296
00:25:23,881 --> 00:25:27,180
this will ensure that the chocolate solidifies properly.

297
00:25:27,181 --> 00:25:30,160
What that means is it will have a good shine,

298
00:25:30,161 --> 00:25:35,100
it will be hard, and the appearance will last longer.

299
00:25:35,101 --> 00:25:38,220
So the milk chocolate is right over to this area,

300
00:25:38,221 --> 00:25:40,240
And then it's deposited into molds.

301
00:25:42,480 --> 00:25:47,120
When making almond bars, Hershey carefully controls the number of nut pieces,

302
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:49,920
depositing about seven almonds in each.

303
00:25:52,400 --> 00:25:56,920
The bars are cooled for about 20 to 30 minutes and sent to the packaging area.

304
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:01,560
About 1.9 million bars come off these lines per day.

305
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:07,240
And, of course, there are the Hershey Kisses.

306
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:12,720
and those decadent Reese's peanut butter cups.

307
00:26:17,180 --> 00:26:21,580
The multi-billion dollar company of today was started in 1894

308
00:26:21,581 --> 00:26:28,520
by an American innovator determined to bring Europe's best to the folks at home.

309
00:26:29,260 --> 00:26:32,420
Milton Hershey was aware of the Swiss milk chocolate,

310
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:36,420
so very soon he began experimenting with trying to make milk chocolate.

311
00:26:36,421 --> 00:26:41,420
After a few years, he had an edible product that he introduced in 1900.

312
00:26:41,421 --> 00:26:46,420
When he was developing his formula and was assured of the success,

313
00:26:46,421 --> 00:26:54,420
he began construction of a major new chocolate manufacturing plant here in what is today Hershey, Pennsylvania.

314
00:26:58,420 --> 00:27:02,420
Over a century later, Hershey is synonymous with chocolate.

315
00:27:06,420 --> 00:27:13,740
Hershey's most formidable foe was founded in Tacoma, Washington, by Frank and Ethel

316
00:27:13,741 --> 00:27:14,740
Mars.

317
00:27:14,741 --> 00:27:20,660
The Mars Corporation started in 1911, Frank Mars making candy in his kitchen.

318
00:27:20,661 --> 00:27:26,040
He was very successful and moved to Chicago and built a factory there in 1929, right when

319
00:27:26,041 --> 00:27:27,800
the Depression started.

320
00:27:27,801 --> 00:27:31,700
But still he was able to be successful.

321
00:27:31,701 --> 00:27:35,900
Frank Mars created the Milky Way Bar in 1923.

322
00:27:35,901 --> 00:27:41,940
flavored nougat and caramel enrobed in milk chocolate.

323
00:27:41,941 --> 00:27:46,260
Mars added peanuts to nougat and caramel in 1930.

324
00:27:46,261 --> 00:27:49,500
The result became one of the most popular candy bars,

325
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:53,420
Snickers, with $2 billion in annual retail sales.

326
00:27:57,180 --> 00:27:59,860
Frank's son Forrest continued the family's candy making

327
00:27:59,861 --> 00:28:02,940
tradition and left his own footprint on chocolate history.

328
00:28:02,941 --> 00:28:10,480
While on a trip to Spain during the Spanish Civil War, Forrest encountered soldiers eating

329
00:28:10,481 --> 00:28:17,320
pellets of chocolate encased in a hard shell that kept the candy solid in hot weather.

330
00:28:17,321 --> 00:28:26,220
In 1941, he introduced M&M's, ironically made with chocolate supplied by Hershey.

331
00:28:26,221 --> 00:28:35,880
By the late 1940s, M&M's joined the ranks of American chocolate success stories.

332
00:28:35,881 --> 00:28:40,100
Because the melting point of cocoa butter is just below body temperature, the candy

333
00:28:40,101 --> 00:28:45,920
coating is applied as a barrier to ensure that M&M's will always melt in your mouth,

334
00:28:45,921 --> 00:28:48,820
not in your hand.

335
00:28:48,821 --> 00:28:54,740
M&M's, Snickers and Hershey bars represent just a fraction of the world's demand for

336
00:28:54,741 --> 00:28:59,140
chocolate.

337
00:28:59,141 --> 00:29:05,740
To meet that craving, about 3.3 million tons of cocoa are harvested annually.

338
00:29:05,741 --> 00:29:12,300
But that supply is constantly threatened.

339
00:29:12,301 --> 00:29:17,080
Cacao trees can easily fall prey to a number of fungal diseases that have plagued the crop

340
00:29:17,081 --> 00:29:19,580
for years.

341
00:29:19,581 --> 00:29:21,620
The major problems are moniliasis

342
00:29:21,621 --> 00:29:24,680
and with Chisbrun diseases.

343
00:29:24,681 --> 00:29:27,020
This spot is infected by monilia.

344
00:29:27,021 --> 00:29:28,720
It produces a lesion.

345
00:29:28,721 --> 00:29:32,800
A square centimeter of lesion is able to produce

346
00:29:32,801 --> 00:29:36,620
44 millions of spores.

347
00:29:36,621 --> 00:29:40,120
One spore could equal one lost pod.

348
00:29:40,121 --> 00:29:43,860
Through the math, the effects are devastating.

349
00:29:43,861 --> 00:29:50,420
In the early 1980s, Monelia pod rot decimated three quarters of Costa Rica's cocoa crop.

350
00:29:52,580 --> 00:29:56,580
If a disease of this magnitude hit the major producing region of West Africa,

351
00:29:58,340 --> 00:30:00,900
we could expect to pay a lot more for that Hershey bar.

352
00:30:04,660 --> 00:30:09,860
Geneticists and agroforesters at the Cotier Research Facility in Torrealba, Costa Rica,

353
00:30:10,500 --> 00:30:12,660
are striving to make sure this doesn't happen.

354
00:30:13,860 --> 00:30:21,060
In association with the member-funded World Cocoa Foundation, these researchers are developing

355
00:30:21,061 --> 00:30:24,960
sturdier cocoa plants that will ultimately benefit everyone.

356
00:30:24,961 --> 00:30:31,920
There's an international cooperation between industry organizations, universities, research

357
00:30:31,921 --> 00:30:38,360
institutes and governments involved in producing new hybrids, finding new ways to fight pests

358
00:30:38,361 --> 00:30:42,260
and diseases, and then taking all of that scientific research

359
00:30:42,261 --> 00:30:46,040
and knowledge out to the fields to 40 to 50 million people

360
00:30:46,041 --> 00:30:48,380
who grow cocoa around the world.

361
00:30:48,381 --> 00:30:50,920
And this is not also for the farmers,

362
00:30:50,921 --> 00:30:53,420
but also for the consumers that need

363
00:30:53,421 --> 00:30:58,040
to have this delicious product in their mouth.

364
00:30:58,041 --> 00:30:59,180
He's right.

365
00:30:59,181 --> 00:31:01,340
What could be better than chocolate?

366
00:31:01,341 --> 00:31:03,800
How about chocolate inside of chocolate?

367
00:31:08,360 --> 00:31:17,240
Alfred Hitchcock used Bosco chocolate syrup to simulate blood during the famous shower scene in his 1960 thriller, Psycho.

